  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "zV9akfw0qG/Dj1fIG0BEfGdEYzs=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:36.997Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "adesso SE sits at the intersection the screen is looking for: a public administration business above EUR 200m growing at 12%, and a Camunda practice. That combination \u2014 process orchestration in government, at a firm that already sells into it \u2014 is the shortest path from the thesis to a funded requirement. The assessment agrees, calling it the right intersection.\n\nWhat rules it out is scale relative to Powerhouse's own maturity, at roughly 10,000 staff. This is a criterion about the seller rather than the buyer: an organisation that size requires an alliance process, reference architecture review and a committee, and a first design partner has to be small enough for one delivery lead to decide. The row is not saying adesso is a bad fit; it is saying the fit cannot be executed yet.\n\nThe note records a specific re-entry condition \u2014 revisit with Materna \u2014 which makes this a deferral rather than a rejection. It is also worth reading alongside the separate adesso Group row in the Flowable directory, which describes an 11,000-plus-staff DACH organisation certified across Camunda in Switzerland, Germany and Austria and carried at priority 3 rather than 9. The same group appears twice with different tool attributions and different rankings.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**9 \u00b7 do not pitch**\n- adesso SE (DE \u00b7 approx 10,000)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> Public administration >EUR 200m and growing 12%, plus Camunda. Right intersection, wrong size for your maturity. Revisit with Materna.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:28:36.997686Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:36.997690Z"
    }
  },
